.\" -*-nroff-*-
.TH POWWOW 6 2026-09-28 "powwow @PACKAGE_VERSION@"
.SH NAME
powwow \- telnet protocol client for MUD playing
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B powwow
.RI "[" options "] [" definition-file "] [" host " " port "]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B powwow
is a client program that implements the telnet protocol, adding some
bells and whistles to the standard
.BR telnet (1)
communication utility, mainly intended for playing multi-user games
such as
.IR MUDs " (" "Multi-User Dungeons" ")."
.PP
.B powwow
is either invoked with
.I host
and
.IR port ,
and then does not save any definitions, or with a
.IR definition-file ,
which is supposed to contain various definitions as well as the name
of the host and the port number.  If the definition-file does not
exist, the user is prompted for the host name and port number which
are saved in the new file.  The definition file can be edited with any
text editor, and is updated whenever a definition (action, alias,
etc.) is changed.  If the environment variable
.B POWWOWDIR
is defined,
.B powwow
will look there first for definition files (and create new ones
there).
.PP
Lines that begin with \[oq]#\[cq] are assumed to be special commands
to be processed locally.  Help is available with the
.I #help
command; the complete manual is the file
.IR powwow.doc ,
installed in the same directory as the help text.  Notably,
semicolons (\[oq];\[cq]) separate commands on a single line.  To send
a literal semicolon, prefix it with a backslash: \[oq]\e;\[cq].
.PP
.B powwow
also supports the MUME editing protocol for editing texts locally.
The name of the editor is taken from the environment variable
.B POWWOWEDITOR
or
.BR EDITOR ;
.BR vi (1)
is used by default.  For text viewing,
.B POWWOWPAGER
is searched, then
.B PAGER
and
.BR more (1)
is used by default.
.PP
If the first character of the editor is \[oq]&\[cq] (which is then
skipped), the editor is assumed to run in another window and
.B powwow
will run concurrently with the editor without waiting for it to
finish.
.PP
When launched, the editor program will have its
.B TITLE
environment variable set to the document title sent from the MUD.
.PP
.B powwow
handles text internally as Unicode (UTF-8), and converts it to and
from the character set of the terminal's locale.  The connection to the
server can use UTF-8 (the default), ISO-8859-1 or US-ASCII, as negotiated
using the telnet CHARSET option (RFC 2066) or selected with
.BR \-\-latin1 .
Invalid input is shown as U+FFFD; characters that cannot be represented
are shown or sent as \[oq]?\[cq].
Captured, recorded and edited files use the connection's character set;
definition files are UTF-8, and older ISO-8859-1 ones are converted when
loaded.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
display help text and exit
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\fR[=\fItls-options\fR]
use encrypted TLS connections (the default); this overrides the TLS
setting of the definition file's host; additional
.IR tls-options ,
separated by colons (\[oq]:\[cq]), are:
.RS
.TP
.B no-verify
do not validate the CA certificate
.TP
.BI host= HOST
assume the host name to be
.I HOST
when validating the CA certificate
.RE
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-tls
use unencrypted connections; this overrides the TLS setting of the
definition file's host
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-latin1
start the connection in ISO-8859-1 and prefer ISO-8859-1 when negotiating
the character set with the server
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-utf8
start the connection in UTF-8 and prefer UTF-8 when negotiating the
character set with the server (the default); this overrides the character
set of the definition file's host
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
display the
.B powwow
version and exit; given twice, also display the credits and the options
.B powwow
was compiled with
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM
Your terminal type.
.TP
.B POWWOWDIR
Where
.B powwow
will store and search for its files by default.
.TP
.BR POWWOWEDITOR " or " EDITOR
Name of editor to use.
.TP
.BR POWWOWPAGER " or " PAGER
Name of pager to use.
.SH FILES
.TP \w'$\[lC]POWWOWDIR\[rC]/*\ \ \ 'u
powwow
The binary.
.TP
$\[lC]POWWOWDIR\[rC]/*
Definition files for aliases, etc.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Messages from
.B powwow
are prefixed with a \[oq]#\[cq] to separate them from the output
of the remote host.
.SH BUGS
Report bugs and send patches to the maintainer; see
.BR AUTHORS .
.SH AUTHORS
.MT max@Linuz.sns.it
Massimiliano Ghilardi
.ME
and Gustav H\[oa]llberg are the people that added many new features
to an existing client,
.B Cancan
and transformed it into
.BR powwow .
.MT f91-men@nada.kth.se
Mattias Engdeg\[oa]rd
.ME
was the original author of Cancan but many other people have
contributed at least as much, including
.MT finnag@pvv.unit.no
Finn Arne Gangstad
.ME ,
.MT dgay@di.epfl.ch
David Gay
.ME ,
.MT gdezern@satelnet.org
Gary Dezern
.ME
and
.MT vivriel@scs.com.sg
Lai-Chang Woo
.ME .
.PP
.B powwow
moved to hoopajoo.net in 2005, and is now maintained by
.MT bpk@hoopajoo.net
Steve Slaven
.ME ;
please send bug reports and patches to that address.
.PP
.UR http://hoopajoo.net/projects/powwow.html
The
.B powwow
WWW page
.UE
has more information.
.PP
.B powwow
was originally inspired by Peter Unold's
.I Tintin
client, but now only bears a very remote resemblance to the original.
It aims to be a simple but very interactive client, and contains
features that are interesting for \[lq]immortal\[rq] players, in
particular on MUME, though any mud player should benefit from
.B powwow
as well.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR telnet (1),
.BR more (1),
.BR vi (1)
